<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Read this first</p>
    <h2 id="t-the-question-physics-does-not-touch">The Question Physics Does Not Touch</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every physical theory opens its account with the curtain already raised: a stage already
      standing, laws already in force, fields already humming. Why there is a stage at all is set aside as
      metaphysics. This framework refuses that surrender &mdash; and answers with geometry rather than
      with a deeper particle or an earlier instant.</p>

    <p>Take zero and one. Zero is not merely nothing; it is the floor no shrinking quantity ever reaches,
      approached forever and never arrived at &mdash; an infinity opening <em>downward</em>, into the small.
      One is the edge of the measurable, the threshold of <em>this</em> rather than <em>that</em>. Between
      these two unremarkable markers lies something stranger than either: the interval from zero to one
      contains more numbers than the entire unending march of 1, 2, 3, 4&hellip; put together.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Cantor proved it. The framework takes that asymmetry not as a
      curiosity but as a tilt built into the foundations &mdash; one whose consequences return, much later,
      as the direction of time itself.</p>

    <p>Then the decisive question: <strong>how are zero and one joined?</strong> A straight line from the one
      toward the infinitely small never closes. For the relation to become something real the path must
      curve, and the constant that governs all curving is <strong>π</strong>. In this reading π is not a
      number discovered inside a universe already running. It is the first condition of there being any
      universe to run.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      e^{i\pi}+1=0
    \]</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading throughout: <span class="sci">&#9670; established science</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship and history</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; speculative and contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

